                <pb facs="0185" n="73" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg73"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 21158/2.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé of Austria-Hungary.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1910-03-24"><hi rendition="#italic">March 24,
                        1910</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 512.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of the 10th instant, referring to previous correspondence and
                    making inquiry as to the status of the citizenship case of Rudolf Warren-Lippit
                    and his son.</p>
                <p>In reply I have the honor to inform you that the matter has again been brought to
                    the attention of the American Embassy at Vienna, with instructions for an early
                    report.</p>
                <p>Assuring you that the result of the embassy’s investigation will be immediately
                    communicated to you upon its receipt,</p>
                <p>I avail, etc.,</p>
                <closer>(For <signed>Mr. Knox</signed>),<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Huntington Wilson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
